  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of the Treasury has obligated $6.7B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The department recorded 8,211 awards with an average award size of $817K, and obligations fell 21.6% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations peaked at $10.3B in FY23 and then trended lower to $9.3B in FY24, $8.6B in FY25, and $6.7B in FY26. The largest spending categories so far in FY2026 show a mix of metals processing and IT services: Nonferrous Metal (except Aluminum) Smelting and Refining received $1.5B and Other Computer Related Services received $1.2B. Treasury procurement also includes Computer Systems Design Services at $720M, and several metals, software, and support categories receiving smaller but still material amounts. The top vendors reflect that mix. STONEX FINANCIAL LTD received $1.3B, ASAHI REFINING USA INC received $603M, and multiple technology and manufacturing firms received contracts in the hundreds of millions of dollars.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Treasury contract spending changed recently?",
        "body": "The Department of the Treasury obligated $6.7B in FY2026 to date, down 21.6% year over year. Obligations were $8.6B in FY25, $9.3B in FY24, $10.3B in FY23, $9.3B in FY22, and $9.6B in FY21. The award count for FY2026 to date is 8,211 with an average award size of $817K. These figures show a decline in total obligations compared with the recent peak in FY23."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What goods and services and vendors dominate Treasury contracts?",
        "body": "Nonferrous Metal (except Aluminum) Smelting and Refining is the largest category at $1.5B, followed by Other Computer Related Services at $1.2B and Computer Systems Design Services at $720M. Other notable categories include Nonferrous Metal (except Copper and Aluminum) Rolling, Drawing, and Extruding at $610M, and Custom Computer Programming Services at $322M. STONEX FINANCIAL LTD leads vendors with $1.3B, and ASAHI REFINING USA INC is next with $603M; several other vendors received between $87.3M and $277M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of the Treasury spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of the Treasury has obligated $6.7B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The department recorded 8,211 awards with an average award size of $817K, and obligations fell 21.6% year over year. Fiscal obligations were $8.6B in FY25 and peaked at $10.3B in FY23."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Treasury buy?",
      "a": "Treasury buys both metals processing and information technology services. The largest NAICS category is Nonferrous Metal (except Aluminum) Smelting and Refining at $1.5B, Other Computer Related Services at $1.2B, and Computer Systems Design Services at $720M, plus categories such as Custom Computer Programming Services at $322M and Software Publishers at $125M."
    },
    {
      "q": "Who are Treasury's largest contractors?",
      "a": "STONEX FINANCIAL LTD is Treasury's largest contractor at $1.3B. Other top vendors include ASAHI REFINING USA INC at $603M, FCN, INC. at $277M, and ACCENTURE FEDERAL SERVICES LLC at $276M, with additional vendors receiving between $87.3M and $277M."
